NNAAMMEE
       pamgetcolor  -  display the average colors from specified regions in an
       image.


SSYYNNOOPPSSIISS
       ppaammggeettccoolloorr [--ffoorrmmaatt _f_o_r_m_a_t] [--rraaddiiuuss _r_a_d_i_u_s] [--lliinneeaarr]  [--iinnffiillee  _p_a_m_‐
       _f_i_l_e] _r_e_g_i_o_n_1 [_r_e_g_i_o_n_2 ...]


DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
       This program is part of NNeettppbbmm(1).

       ppaammggeettccoolloorr  prints  the average colors of a set of circular regions in
       the input image.

       You specify a  region  as  a  positional  argument  of  the  form  _c_o_l_‐
       _u_m_n,_r_o_w[:_l_a_b_e_l], where _c_o_l_u_m_n and _r_o_w are the coordinates of the center
       of the circle (first row of pixels is row 0; leftmost column of  pixels
       is  column  0), and _l_a_b_e_l an optional label that ppaammggeettccoolloorr shall dis‐
       play to identify that region in its output.  All regions have  he  same
       radius,  specified  by the --rraaddiiuuss option.  The region centers must lie
       within the image, but part of a region  may  fall  outside  the  image;
       ppaammggeettccoolloorr  considers only the part that is within the image in calcu‐
       lations.



EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
       To read the color of the pixel at location (10,14) in the default  for‐
       mat:

           pamgetcolor 10,14 -infile test.ppm


       To  read  the colors of three pixels in the default format, assigning a
       label to each pixel:

           pamgetcolor 10,10:topleft 100,100:middle 200,200:bottomright -infile test.ppm


       To read with 16-bit precision the average color in the  circle  with  a
       radius of four pixels and the center at (100,100):

           pamgetcolor -format int:65535 -radius 4 100,100 -infile test.ppm




OOPPTTIIOONNSS
       In  addition  to  the options common to all programs based on libnetpbm
       (most notably --qquuiieett, see
        Common Options ⟨index.html#commonoptions⟩  ),  ppaammggeettccoolloorr  recognizes
       the following command line options:




       --ffoorrmmaatt
              This  specifies  the  format in which to output the colors.  The
              output    is    always    a    Netpbm    color     specification
              ⟨libppm.html#colorname⟩ ; this format tells which of the various
              specifications for the same color the program uses.

              This argument is of the form  _f_o_r_m_a_t_I_d[:_p_a_r_a_m],  where  _f_o_r_m_a_t_I_d
              specifies  the  format and _p_a_r_a_m is a positive integer parameter
              that, depending on _f_o_r_m_a_t_I_d, indicates either precision or  nor‐
              malization. The following values are possible for _f_o_r_m_a_t_I_d:


       int    Samples  are decimal integers normalized to the maxval specified
              by _p_a_r_a_m.  Example: rrggbb--225555::225555//112288//6644 This format  is  the  de‐
              fault, with a maxval of 255.

       norm   Samples  are  floating  point numbers normalized to unity.  E.g.
              rrggbbii::11..00//00..55//..2255 _p_a_r_a_m specifies precision as the number of dig‐
              its in the fractional part.

       x11    Samples   are   hexadecimal  numbers  with  _p_a_r_a_m  digits,  e.g.
              rrggbb::0011//ffff//88000000



       --rraaddiiuuss
              sets the radius of the regions.  A value of zero causes  ppaammggeett‐‐
              ccoolloorr to measure a single pixel and is the default.


       --iinnffiillee
              This specifies the Netpbm file to analyze.

              If  you  don't  specify this option, ppaammggeettccoolloorr reads the image
              from Standard Input.


       --lliinneeaarr
              This tells ppaammggeettccoolloorr to work with the intensity-linear  varia‐
              tion  of  Netpbm  images  where  the samples are proportional to
              light intensity rather than to brightness, as in true (gamma-ad‐
              justed) Netpbm formats.




